WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.700
If you've seen the Rat Queen from Netflix's&nbsp;
"Cabinet of Curiosities," you'll probably never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:11.280
forget it. Here's how the creative team brought&nbsp;
the creature to life — without digital effects.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.120
Halloween may have come and gone this&nbsp;
year, but "Guillermo del Toro's Cabinet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.920
of Curiosities" isn't going anywhere. You&nbsp;
can still have a spooky good time with it.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:25.200
If you're unfamiliar with the Netflix series,&nbsp;
it's an anthology of eight spooky tales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.280
in the vein of "Tales From the Crypt"&nbsp;
or "Masters of Horror." A brief glance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.520
through the cast list proves that,&nbsp;
while big celebrity talent might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.000
show up for all horror projects,&nbsp;
everyone shows up for del Toro.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.400
The curiosities vary drastically, featuring&nbsp;
everything from demonic storage units to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.740
alien-infested corpses to ghostly forests&nbsp;
to rodents of unusual size. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.280
only one of those abominations was&nbsp;
brought to life with only practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.660
effects. Unfortunately, it was the&nbsp;
rat, and that thing is terrifying.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:00.000
In the second episode, simply titled "Graveyard&nbsp;
Rats," a down-on-his-luck grave robber played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.720
by David Hewlett can't seem to hold&nbsp;
onto any trinkets that he unearths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.363
because an ungodly swarm of rats snatches&nbsp;
the treasures away the moment he finds them.

00:01:08.363 --> 00:01:10.170
"As you can see, I'm at my wit's end!"

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.760
He can't seem to really take the hint, though,&nbsp;
and continues to rob graves. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.740
he stumbles across what the show's art team&nbsp;
casually referred to as the Rat Queen. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.540
the term "queen" may imply an aura of grace,&nbsp;
the Rat Queen has none of that. It does have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.160
what you might call some royal authority,&nbsp;
though. It's a truly intimidating creature.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:35.520
She's a monstrous beast of an animatronic puppet&nbsp;
with bulbous, milky eyes and wispy fur that hardly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.260
covers her mottled skin. Next to Hewlett, the&nbsp;
Rat Queen puppet is more than human-sized;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:46.260
it's basically the size of a horse. Vincenzo&nbsp;
Natali, who directed the episode, told "Newsweek"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.700
that the Rat Queen was something of a miracle,&nbsp;
even if it looked like a nightmare. He said:

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.160
"It is the first puppet that I've worked with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.420
actually works 100 percent. It was&nbsp;
really quite something to behold."

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.527
"The desire was always to make our creatures&nbsp;
with physical, tangible materials."

00:02:03.527 --> 00:02:07.320
Natali went on to describe all the benefits&nbsp;
of sticking with practical effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.180
because it gave Hewlett something to act&nbsp;
against, which CGI wouldn't do. He said,

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.920
"The queen rat was so shocking, and they&nbsp;
had it right in David's face. I think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.880
was better than if he was acting to a tennis&nbsp;
ball on a grip stand, and it's thrilling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.840
There's something about watching that on a&nbsp;
monitor, when everything's actually in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:30.000
frame and it's happening in real time, that&nbsp;
is exhilarating for everybody. Then you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.600
a subtlety in terms of how that creature&nbsp;
interacts with the light and even little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.340
things like the way its whiskers move and so on,&nbsp;
that would be very difficult to do digitally."

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.240
The team at Spectral Motion, the company&nbsp;
that created the Rat Queen puppet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:48.120
clearly created something very lifelike. But it&nbsp;
can't move on its own, at least not yet. For now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.600
at least, the creature requires a&nbsp;
helping human hand. Natali said,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.160
"The puppeteers were fantastic and we&nbsp;
had a great time. I had a great time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.680
directing the puppet — like the puppet is a&nbsp;
character, it is an actor on that set. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.860
kind of thrilling for me as a director&nbsp;
to interact with a creature like that."

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.140
At least he was having fun.

